{
  "rules": [
    "Mild positive bias at short-to-mid horizons: forecasts tend to run ~0.2–1.0 EUR above realized levels in the first ~1–20 days, especially during steady/rising regimes.",
    "Downturns are under-modeled (lag/insufficient drawdown): when the market rolls over, the forecast path declines too slowly and stays too high for ~1–2 weeks.",
    "Over-smoothing / under-reacting to volatility: forecasts compress day-to-day moves; sharp dips are not deep enough and sharp rebounds are not strong enough.",
    "Late-horizon upside miss after regime shift: beyond ~day 20, forecasts can become too low when a new upswing accelerates (e.g., realized breaks higher while forecast stays range-bound).",
    "Overshoot after local peaks: following short-term spikes, the model often projects continued strength, but realized prices mean-revert sooner; apply a downward adjustment after peak-like conditions."
  ]
}
